        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Atmospheric, Power Vent, or Condensing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Venting determines where the heater can physically go, what the installation costs, and whether it keeps working in a power cut. It is the decision that most constrains your options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Atmospheric Vent</h3>

          <p>
            The traditional design. Hot exhaust rises naturally up a chimney or flue with no fan involved. That makes it the cheapest to buy and install, and the only type that continues producing hot water during a power outage since it needs no electricity at all. The constraint is that you must have a suitable flue, and the space needs enough combustion air, which is a genuine issue in tightly sealed modern basements.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Vent</h3>

          <p>
            A fan pushes exhaust horizontally out through a sidewall using PVC, which frees the heater from needing a chimney and opens up locations atmospheric units cannot serve. The costs are a higher purchase price, a required electrical outlet, audible fan noise, and no hot water production during a blackout.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Direct Vent and Sealed Combustion</h3>

          <p>
            These draw combustion air from outside through a concentric or twin pipe rather than from the room. That makes them appropriate for tightly sealed spaces and safer where the heater shares a confined room with other appliances. They cost more and the venting is more involved.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensing</h3>

          <p>
            Extracts additional heat from the exhaust until moisture condenses out, reaching efficiency no conventional tank matches and cooling the exhaust enough for PVC venting. It requires a condensate drain and costs substantially more, so it suits high-demand households where the efficiency gain is used heavily.
          </p>

