        <!-- SECTION 4: Why Water Heaters Die, and How to Delay It -->
        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Water Heaters Die, and How to Delay It
          </h2>

          <p>
            A glass-lined steel tank full of hot water is a corrosion problem waiting to happen, and essentially every tank heater eventually fails the same way: the lining develops a flaw, the steel beneath it corrodes, and the tank leaks. Two maintenance tasks delay that by years.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The anode rod is the sacrificial part.</strong> A magnesium or aluminium rod hangs inside the tank and corrodes preferentially, protecting the steel. Once it is consumed, corrosion attacks the tank itself. Checking it around year three to five and replacing it when depleted is the single highest-value thing you can do, and it costs a fraction of a new heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sediment is the second killer.</strong> Minerals settle out of the water and accumulate on the tank bottom, directly above the burner on a gas unit. That layer insulates the water from the flame, so the burner runs longer, the steel above it overheats, and the glass lining cracks. It also makes the popping and rumbling noises an old heater produces.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Flush annually.</strong> Attaching a hose to the drain valve and running several gallons out clears loose sediment before it bakes on. In hard water, twice a year is better. This is a fifteen-minute job that meaningfully extends life and preserves efficiency.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set the thermostat to 120 degrees.</strong> Higher temperatures accelerate both corrosion and mineral deposition, cost more in gas, and create a genuine scalding hazard. 120 is hot enough for every household use and is the standard recommendation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expansion tanks matter on closed systems.</strong> If your supply has a check valve or pressure reducer, heated water has nowhere to expand and pressure spikes every cycle, stressing the tank and the relief valve. A small thermal expansion tank absorbs that and is required by code in many areas.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Noise That Means Act Now</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Rumbling, popping, or kettling from a gas water heater is sediment on the tank bottom trapping steam beneath it. It is not cosmetic — it means the burner is overheating the steel through an insulating crust, which is exactly how glass linings crack. Flush the tank promptly, and if flushing does not quiet it, the sediment has hardened and the heater is on borrowed time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Sizing a Gas Tank to Your Household -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing a Gas Tank to Your Household
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One to Two People</h3>

          <p>
            A 30 to 40 gallon gas heater is genuinely sufficient. Gas recovery is fast enough that a 40-gallon tank handles two consecutive showers and a dishwasher without difficulty. Buying larger wastes money on standby loss every day for a capacity that never gets used.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three to Four People</h3>

          <p>
            A 40 to 50 gallon tank with a burner of at least 40,000 BTU. Focus on first hour rating rather than gallons: a well-burnered 40 often serves this household better than a weakly burnered 50, and costs less to run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Five or More, or a Soaking Tub</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to 50 to 75 gallons with a high-output burner. A large soaking tub alone can consume most of a 50-gallon tank in one fill, so if you have one, size for it explicitly rather than treating it as an occasional extra.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Consider the Recovery Pattern, Not Just the Count</h3>

          <p>
            Four people who shower across three hours need far less capacity than two people who shower back to back at seven. Household schedule matters as much as household size, and it is the thing sizing charts ignore.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Physical Fit Before Ordering</h3>

          <p>
            Tank dimensions vary considerably at the same nominal capacity, and taller or wider units may not fit the existing alcove, clear the joists, or come down the stairs. Measure the space and the route, including door widths and turns, before committing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Gas Tank vs Electric Tank vs Tankless -->
        <div id="gas-vs-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas Tank vs Electric Tank vs Tankless
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people replacing a water heater swap like for like, which is usually right but occasionally leaves a better option unconsidered. Here is how the three genuinely compare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Tank</h3>

          <p>
            Recovers several times faster than electric, which is why a 40-gallon gas heater frequently outperforms a 50-gallon electric one in real use. Gas is cheaper per unit of heat in most of the country, and atmospheric models keep working in a blackout. The costs are venting requirements, combustion air, and a modest ongoing standby loss.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Tank</h3>

          <p>
            Cheaper to buy, simple to install anywhere with adequate service, and requires no venting or gas line at all. It is also the only sensible tank option in a home with no gas supply. The drawbacks are slow recovery, higher running cost in most regions, and complete dependence on power. Heat pump electric models flip the running-cost equation but cost far more and need a warm space with airflow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tankless</h3>

          <p>
            Endless hot water, no standby loss, a much smaller footprint, and roughly double the service life. What you give up is instant delivery, simplicity of installation, and unlimited simultaneous demand, since capacity is a flow rate rather than a stored volume. Installation costs frequently exceed the unit price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose Between Them</h3>

          <p>
            If your complaint is running out of hot water during back-to-back showers, tankless solves it directly. If your complaint is a slow reheat between loads, a gas tank with a stronger burner fixes it for far less money. And if you have no gas supply, the realistic comparison is between an electric tank and a heat pump unit rather than anything on this page.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Replacement Timing Question</h3>

          <p>
            Water heaters rarely fail gracefully. Once a tank is past ten years, showing rust at the fittings, or rumbling persistently after a flush, planning a replacement beats waiting for it. An emergency swap limits you to whatever is in stock locally, which is exactly how people end up with the wrong size or the wrong vent type.
          </p>
        </div>

