        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes with an existing chimney, the A.O. Smith Signature 100 50-gallon offers the best combination of recovery, price, and parts availability. Where there is no chimney, the Rheem Performance Platinum power vent adds sidewall venting plus automatic leak shutoff. For longevity, the Bradford White RG240T6N is what plumbers tend to install in their own homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size gas water heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One to two people are well served by 30 to 40 gallons, three to four by 40 to 50 with a burner of at least 40,000 BTU, and larger households or homes with a soaking tub by 50 to 75. Compare first hour rating rather than gallons, since a strong burner on a smaller tank often beats a weak burner on a larger one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is first hour rating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is how many gallons of hot water the heater can deliver in one hour starting fully heated, combining stored volume with how fast the burner reheats as you draw it down. It appears on the yellow EnergyGuide label and predicts real-world performance far better than tank capacity alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a gas water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically eight to twelve years for a glass-lined tank. Lifespan depends heavily on water hardness and maintenance: replacing the anode rod when it is consumed, usually around year three to five, and flushing sediment annually can add several years. Neglecting both routinely cuts a heater life in half.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gas water heaters work during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Atmospheric vent models with a standing pilot do, because they need no electricity at all — the exhaust rises naturally up the flue. Power vent, direct vent, and condensing models use an electric fan and will stop producing hot water when the power goes out, even though gas is still available.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my water heater making popping noises?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sediment has accumulated on the tank bottom directly above the burner, trapping steam beneath it. It is a warning rather than a quirk: the burner is now overheating the steel through an insulating layer, which cracks the glass lining. Flush the tank promptly, and if the noise persists the sediment has hardened.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Gas Water Heater Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tank heaters are simple appliances, and most of what shortens their life is neglect of two maintenance tasks that take under an hour a year.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping by Gallons Instead of First Hour Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two 50-gallon heaters can deliver very different amounts of hot water in a busy hour, because recovery rate matters as much as storage. The first hour rating on the EnergyGuide label combines both. Comparing tank size alone routinely leads people to buy a larger, more expensive heater that performs worse than a smaller one with a stronger burner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Replacing the Anode Rod</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The anode rod exists to corrode so the tank does not. Once it is consumed, usually somewhere around year three to five, corrosion moves to the steel and the clock starts on a leak. Checking and replacing it costs a fraction of a new heater and is the single most effective way to extend one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Sediment Until It Rumbles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Minerals settle onto the tank bottom right above the burner and form an insulating crust, so the burner overheats the steel and cracks the glass lining. By the time it is audibly popping, damage is already underway. Flush annually, or twice a year in hard water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the Thermostat Too High</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Turning it up past 120 degrees accelerates corrosion and scale, wastes gas continuously, and creates a real scalding risk, particularly with children in the house. 120 is hot enough for every domestic use including dishwashing, and it is the standard safety recommendation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

